From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sat, 10 Dec 1994 19:30:33 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-422] Corporate Liability

>From: tburns@mason1.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
>Date: 	Thu, 8 Dec 1994 14:35:09 -1000
>Subject: [#94-12-349] Extropian (?) Fiction
>
>At 9:06 PM 12/8/94, Craig Presson wrote:
>>The modern corporation is the creature of the State.
>>Without the corporate charter, the directors of an enterprise would
>>be severally liable for all of their actions, and without government
>>intervention, would not be able to get by with many of their more
>>questionable practices, or maintain the barriers to competition that
>>many depend on.
>
>I disagree. There's nothing impossible about limited liability under common
>law. If I (and my protection agency) announce in advance that my
>liabilities are limited somehow, it's up to my potential creditors whether
>or not they will do business with me, and the market can speak.

You're thinking of contractual liability.  Insofar as liability is contractual,
you're right.  But there's also tort liability, and when it comes to tort
liability you're wrong.  If a corporation with limited liability incurs tort
liability that exceeds its assets, the victims are Shit Outta Luck - and they
never agreed to the corp.'s limited liability charter.

>...My
>impression of the advent of limited liability is that nobody had to ram it
>down anyone's throat.

Sort of.  Queen Elizabeth I of England, if memory serves, didn't need to be
coerced into granting a limited-liability charter to the British East India
Company so that its investors (which included her) would could only incur tort
liability up to the amount of their investment, giving it a freer hand to incur
such liabilities in its trade with India than it would've had otherwise.  On
the other hand, the victims of its torts didn't agree to the deal.

You don't need limited-liability charters from the Crown or the State in order
for investors' liability to be limited to the amount of their investment.  You
can have trusts in which the trustees contractually assume the additional risk
themselves, or join-stock companies in which liability is assumed by an insurer
in exchange for a liability insurance premium paid out of the firm's profits.

