From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Sat, 10 Dec 1994 19:58:00 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-424] Old-Growth Forestry

First, congrats to Phil Goetz for raising the level of this discussion above
"the Basics."

>From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
>Date: Fri, 9 Dec 1994 12:22:35 -0500
>Subject: [#94-12-375] Summary: Old-Growth Forestry
>
>Eric Forsman, the biologist who started the spotted owl affair, said
>
>...The spotted owl lives only in old-growth forests.

False.  The spotted owl lives in all sorts of forests up and down the Sierra
Nevada mountain range near the western coast of the North American continent,
from California to Washington.  The Northern Spotted Owl, a subspecies of
spotted owls, lives only north of CA, but it, too, has been found to live in
non-old-growth forest.  In fact, a logging company did a study to prove this
only to have some of the non-old growth forest it controlled declared off-
limits to logging in order to protect the subspecies, which wasn't supposed
to be able to live there in the first place.

This kibbutzing over Northern Spotted Owls is a bit beside the point to begin
with.  For one thing, the only way to tell it from any other Spotted Owl is
by the density of spots on its wings, of which there is a gradient from South
to North (the further South you go, the less spot density; the further North,
the more).  The real issue is land.  The Northern Spotted Owl is used as a
proxy for the nationalization of land under the Endangered Species Act, which
specifies that any land which serves as the habitat for an endangered _sub-
species_ can be subjected to de facto nationalization without just compensation
for anyone who may have bought the land in good faith.

>3.  HOW CAN WE MANAGE THEM?
>
>Despite the insistence of Tim Starr, primary forests do not need
>our management.  They were the dominant forests for a long time
>before humans came along.

Hey, I wasn't the only one insisting that.  A new guy from Norway was, too.

>Several people said old woods were destined to burn anyway, implying
>that we might as well cut them down, or that old growth is unnatural,
>"too old", and unstable without human management.  The facts say otherwise:
>climax forests were everywhere before men cut them down.

First, you say that "primary forests" were dominant long before we came
along, now you say that "climax forests were everywhere before men cut them
down."  Are the terms synonymous, or have you contradicted yourself?

Secondly, what evidence is there that either were present in the Kalahari,
Gobi, Sahara, Mojave, & Great Salt Lake deserts before men cut 'em all down
& made 'em into the deserts they are today?  What evidence is there that
the Himalayas, Mongolian Steppes, Urals, Alps, Rockies, & Andes were covered
with 'em before men cut 'em all down?  What evidence is there that the
Serengeti & Great Plains were covered with either primary or climax forests
before us evil men came along & clearcut merrily away?  Or did you state
the case too strongly when you said that they were "everywhere before men
cut them down"?  Did you mean to say that everywhere human explorers found
_forests_ the kind they found were "primary/climax"?

If there isn't any evidence in such places as I've named (not to mention
the oceans & Antarctica) of such forests prior to the advent of man, then
we don't really have any way of telling what might have been there.  For
all we know, it could've been climax forest which wasted away to desert for
lack of rational management.

>The impact of a fire is not the same as the impact of clearcutting.
>Large trees can survive fire.

If so, then the very same weaknesses you previously mentioned as reasons why
they shouldn't be rationally managed would apply to big trees that survived
fires: they'd be more likely to blow over & be pushed over by snow.

>...The giant sequoia Tim mentioned is
>particularly fire-adapted.  It has pitchless, poor-burning bark up to 1 foot
>thick, and can send out new branches from all along its trunk if its
>branches are burned off.  (It can reproduce without fire, but not as well.)
>(Harvey et. al., p. 64-68)

And it doesn't rot very quickly, either, which means that the forest floor in
Giant Sequoia forests get covered with vegetation that doesn't decay well
enough to support sprouts & seedlings very well unless occasional fires assist
the process.  That's another reason why they need fires to help 'em replenish
themselves.

>...Climax forests can benefit from fire management,
>to avoid large fires like the Chinese Black Dragon fire of 1987
>or the Yellowstone fires of 1988, but this could be done by creating
>firebreaks.  "Management" (meaning logging) is not fire management.
>I found no evidence that selective logging reduces the risk of fire.
>Perhaps a selective logging program aimed at minimizing fire risk could
>be developed.  If it preserved a habitat similar to old-growth forest,
>I would be in favor of it.  I wish such a scheme could compete economically
>with other logging schemes, but that seems unlikely.

One point to bring in here is the fact that property taxes on privately held
forest tend to make it more necessary for the land to be developed in some
way if only to pay the taxes.  My grandparents used to harvest a truckload
of cherry trees each year to sell to the Ethan Allen furniture company to
pay their property taxes.  They wouldn't have had to do that if there weren't
any property taxes.  I'm not sure, but I strongly suspect that the property
tax liability involved with large landholdings is a big part of what leads
the Nature Conservancy to donate its acquisitions to the State.

