><Also, we get for free a dictum against violence:  destruction increases
>entropy, which is the inverse of complexity.>
>
>But capitalism is "creative destruction," if nothing were ever destroyed, we
>would be in the neo-Gnostic physicist's heaven, thermodynamic heat death of the
>universe.

A couple of people have picked up on this.  As I said, the measurement of
complexity is complex.  If you study complexity, you know that
complexity seems to be maximal in systems where sudden changes have a 
power-law distribution, meaning in part that small changes are frequent,
and big (destructive) changes are infrequent.  Occasional destruction is
necessary to create complexity; the trick is in walking the tightrope
between order and chaos.  There's a whole field of study on this; I
refer interested people to anything published by the Santa Fe Institute
(marketed through Addison Wesley), esp. Artificial Life II.  Also some
stuff in back issues of Physica D.

>Who granted the individuals rights?  Who enforced them?  Who draws the lines
>around individuals now?  (repeated 4 times)

I originally was thinking only of theoretical ethics,
not how to pragmatically apply them.
If you need to state who is making judgement on actions, then your ethics
is observer-relative.  When I spoke of complexity, I had in mind an absolute
system of morality, meaning it has some objective basis, and it doesn't
matter who makes the observation as long as they measure complexity the
same way.

