From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
Date: Sun, 11 Dec 1994 00:49:45 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-429] SING: Post-singularity morals

>[Nancy Lebovitz]
>>[Me]
>>I think such a moral imperative is compatible with Nietzschianism.
>>It is a variant in which, instead of desiring the production of one strong
>>and beautiful individual as king of the hill, you desire a strong and
>>beautiful universe.
>
>From what I understand of Nietzsche, he wanted each person to pursue
>what they cared about most. 

You get a lot of leeway in interpreting Nietzsche.  First, he had ideas
that are orthogonal.  For instance, I think the doctrine
of eternal recurrence is nonsense, and I can discard it without
compromising his other ideas.  Second, he spoke on two levels:
he talked about the need for someone to create a set of moral beliefs
that the people would follow, and he also talked about his own ideas
of what such a good set of moral beliefs would be (although he never
admitted that these two topics were separate; he wanted to regard his
own version of morals as self-evident).  Third, he worried more about
the big picture than about being precise in the details.

